:"ஆய்","lemma":"ஆய்","gloss":"to become"},{"text":"இன்","lemma":"இன்","gloss":"conditional"}],"gloss":"if"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"நேரார்","parts":[{"text":"நேர்","lemma":"நேர்","gloss":"to oppose"},{"text":"ஆர்","lemma":"ஆர்","gloss":"negative pronominal suffix"}],"gloss":"enemies"},{"surface":"பல்லுயிர்","parts":[{"text":"பல்","lemma":"பல்","gloss":"many"},{"text":"உயிர்","lemma":"உயிர்","gloss":"life"}],"gloss":"many lives"},{"surface":"பருகி","parts":[{"text":"பருகு","lemma":"பருகு","gloss":"to drink"},{"text":"இ","lemma":"இ","gloss":"verbal participle"}],"gloss":"having drunk"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"ஆர்குவை","parts":[{"text":"ஆர்","lemma":"ஆர்","gloss":"to be satisfied"},{"text":"கு","lemma":"கு","gloss":"future marker"},{"text":"ஐ","lemma":"ஐ","gloss":"second person singular"}],"gloss":"you would have been satisfied"},{"surface":"மன்னோ","parts":[{"text":"மன்","lemma":"மன்","gloss":"particle of regret"},{"text":"ஓ","lemma":"ஓ","gloss":"emphasis"}],"gloss":"alas"},{"surface":"அவன்","parts":[{"text":"அவன்","lemma":"அவன்","gloss":"his"}],"gloss":"his"},{"surface":"அமர்அடு","parts":[{"text":"அமர்","lemma":"அமர்","gloss":"battle"},{"text":"அடு","lemma":"அடு","gloss":"to kill"}],"gloss":"battle-killing"},{"surface":"களத்தே","parts":[{"text":"களத்தே","lemma":"களத்தே","gloss":"on the field"}],"gloss":"on the field","split_unverified":true}]}],"literal":"While the calf-loving herd of cows abides in the forest, / and travelers on harsh paths dwell in their desired lands, / and the grain heaps abundant on the threshing floors remain without protection, / because of his righteous scepter that warded off opposing enemies, / and his bright sword of illustrious deeds praised by the world, / when the unfailing Ezhini reached the battlefield, / like an infant abandoned by its parents, / his beloved kinsmen lamented group by group, / with hearts filled with abundant misery distressed by severe hunger, / and even more than this world that remains suffering in pain, / you have lost greatly, O unrighteous Death! / Like a farmer of a ruined family who, not knowing the fertility of the field coming from cultivation, does not eat, / if you had drunk the many lives of his enemies / on his battle-killing field, / you would have been satisfied indeed!","translation":"Under his righteous, unwavering rule, the herds of cows and their calves rested safely in the forest, travelers journeyed and stayed wherever they wished, and abundant heaps of grain lay unguarded on the threshing floors. But now, the truthful Ezhini, whose bright sword was praised by the world, has fallen on the battlefield. Like infants abandoned by their parents, his beloved kinsmen weep in groups. The entire world, distressed by hunger and deep sorrow, suffers. Yet, O unrighteous Death, you have lost far more than this grieving world! You are like a foolish farmer of a ruined household who does not understand the future yield of his fertile field and starves. If only you had waited and drunk the lives of his many enemies on his battlefield, you would have feasted to your heart's content!","modern":"கன்றுகளை விரும்பும் பசுக்கூட்டங்கள் காட்டில் தங்கி வாழவும், வெப்பமான வழிகளில் செல்லும் புதியவர்கள் தாம் விரும்பிய இடங்களில் தங்கி வாழவும், நெற்களங்களில் குவிந்துள்ள நெற்குவியல்கள் காவல் இல்லாமல் கிடக்கவும் வழிசெய்தது அவனது செங்கோல். பகைவர்களை நீக்கி, உலகத்தார் போற்றும் ஒளி பொருந்திய வாளினை ஏந்திய எழினி போர்க்களத்தில் வீழ்ந்தான். பெற்றோரால் கைவிடப்பட்ட குழந்தையைப் போல அவனது சுற்றத்தார் அழுது புலம்புகின்றனர். பசியும் துன்பமும் நிறைந்த உலகை விட, அறமற்ற கூற்றமே! நீதான் பெரும் இழப்பை அடைந்தாய். உழவின் மூலம் வரும் விளைச்சலை அறியாமல் பட்டினியால் வாடும் ஏழை உழவனைப் போல நீ இருக்கிறாய். எழினியின் போர்க்களத்தில் அவனது பகைவர்களின் பல உயிர்களைக் குடித்து நீ பசியாறியிருக்கலாமே!","explanation":"The poet Aricil Kiḻār laments the death of the chieftain Atiyamāṉ Takaṭūr Porutu Vīḻnta Eḻiṉi, addressing the unrighteous god of Death (Kūṟṟam). The poem begins by describing the peace and prosperity of the land under Ezhini's righteous rule, where even unguarded grain heaps were safe. Now that Ezhini has fallen in battle, his subjects and kinsmen are left orphaned and starving, like abandoned infants. The poet rebukes Death, saying that Death has made a foolish mistake. The poet compares Death to an ignorant farmer of a ruined family who starves because he does not understand that letting the crop grow yields a greater harvest. If Death had spared Ezhini, Ezhini would have slain countless enemies in future battles, providing Death with an endless feast of lives.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}